      "math_academy_id": "e-4027",
      "problem": "What are the solutions to $x^2 + 16 = 0?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx^2 + 16 &= 0 \\[3pt]\n\t\tx^2 &= -16\n}\n$$\n\nBy taking the square root, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = \\pm \\sqrt{-16}.\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $x$ using the imaginary unit $\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\, \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pm \\sqrt{-16} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{16\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{16}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x = \\pm 4 \\textrm{i}.$",
